//! lotus/tube.rye -- ALES82: the asymmetric tube drive -- a different ceiling per sign, the DRIVE family's first CLIP that is not odd. The hard clip
//! (ALES78), the overdrive (ALES79), and the wavefolder (ALES80) all shape the sample's MAGNITUDE and carry its SIGN -- they are ODD functions, so a
//! symmetric input drives to a symmetric output and only ODD harmonics arise. The tube drive breaks that symmetry on purpose: it pins the positive
//! half of the wave at one ceiling and the negative half at another. A real vacuum-tube stage clips harder on one polarity than the other, and that
//! uneven clipping is exactly where a tube's warm EVEN-order harmonics are born -- the second harmonic a symmetric transistor clip never makes.
//!
//! The crux is that ONE CEILING PER SIGN IS THE PLAINEST ASYMMETRY THERE IS, and a hard clip is the tube drive whose two ceilings are EQUAL. ALES78
//! pins every boosted sample to the same magnitude both signs; this rung splits that one ceiling into ceil_pos for the positive half and ceil_neg for
//! the negative, pinning each half to its own: boosted = divTrunc(x-num, den) (ALES78's pre-gain, verbatim), then y = pin(boosted, +ceil_pos,
//! −ceil_neg). When ceil_pos == ceil_neg the two branches collapse into ALES78's single ceiling and the tube drive reproduces the hard clip
//! byte-for-byte -- the family recognition, and the honest proof that this rung only GENERALIZES, never replaces. When they differ, the map is NOT
//! odd: tube(1000) = ceil_pos while tube(−1000) = −ceil_neg ≠ −ceil_pos, so a symmetric input drives to an asymmetric output -- a DC offset and a full
//! set of EVEN harmonics. Stated positively: THE UNEVEN RAIL IS THE TUBE'S HONEST EVEN-HARMONIC SIGNATURE, the sibling of ALES81's not-odd map (the
//! bit-crush floors downward, the tube clips unevenly; neither dresses itself in a symmetry it does not have).
//!
//! Each ceiling is checked into [1, sample_max] exactly as ALES78's single ceiling, so the pinned output lands in [−ceil_neg, ceil_pos] ⊆
//! [sample_min, sample_max] BEFORE the write: the pin does the bounding, and the saturate that follows is a documented no-op. The pre-gain runs in
//! i64 under ALES78's max_drive_num bound, so x-num never overflows before the pin. The map is MEMORYLESS -- every output depends only on its own
//! input, so the in-place read-then-write at one index touches nothing another step needs.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES82.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-212318_fill-ales82-lotus-tube-drive.md
//! Stands on: lotus/drive.rye (ALES78) -- the pre-gain, the gain faults, and the ceiling law, reused with one ceiling per sign; lotus/fader.rye
//! (ALES6) -- the num/den gain in i64; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_min/max, the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate -- the gain is a rational num/den (unity = 1/1), the two ceilings magnitudes in sample units (not
//! decibels), and the shape a piecewise-constant asymmetric clip, instantaneous (no attack/release, no anti-aliasing -- the harmonics fold in the i16
//! domain exactly as any integer clipper's). One multiply, one divide, one sign-aware pin per sample; the gain runs in i64 so it never overflows
//! before the pin, and the pinned value fits the i16 rail by construction.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/tube.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const drive = @import("drive.rye");

pub const tube_version = "20260814.212318";

/// The asymmetric tube drive's faults, one consistent name each. BadGain, BadCeiling, and BadRange coincide by name with ALES78's hard drive (Zig
/// merges error sets by name) -- so a composer sees one consistent fault whichever drive refuses. BadCeiling here guards BOTH ceilings: each side must
/// be a legal magnitude in [1, sample_max], so an asymmetric clip whose either rail is silent or out of reach is refused before any write.
pub const TubeError = error{
    /// A zero denominator, a gain below unity (num < den -- a drive boosts, never attenuates), or a numerator past drive.max_drive_num -- no write.
    BadGain,
    /// Either ceiling below one sample unit (would silence that half) or above sample_max (nothing boosted could be pinned there) -- refused, no write.
    BadCeiling,
    /// A span outside the current samples -- refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Drive `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: each sample is boosted by the rational gain `num`/`den` (at least unity) and then
/// pinned ASYMMETRICALLY -- the positive half to `ceil_pos`, the negative half to `−ceil_neg`. A hard clip is this same map with `ceil_pos == ceil_neg`.
/// After the call every positive output is at most `ceil_pos`, every negative at least `−ceil_neg`, its sign matches the input's, and every output
/// depends only on its own input (the drive is memoryless). Refuses BadGain (ALES78's three gain faults), BadCeiling (either side outside [1,
/// sample_max]), and BadRange -- each BEFORE any write, so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only -- never the length.
pub fn tube(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, num: u32, den: u32, ceil_pos: i32, ceil_neg: i32) TubeError!void {
    // invariant: the gain is legal exactly as ALES78's hard drive -- nonzero denominator, at least unity, numerator within the safe bound. The same
    // three checks, so the same three faults, before any write.
    if (den == 0) return error.BadGain;
    if (num < den) return error.BadGain;
    if (num > drive.max_drive_num) return error.BadGain;
    // invariant: BOTH ceilings are legal magnitudes -- at least one sample unit (a zero ceiling would silence that half) and no greater than the i16
    // rail (a ceiling nothing could reach is not a ceiling). ALES78's exact bound, applied to each side.
    if (ceil_pos < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil_pos > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil_neg < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil_neg > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples -- checked without underflow (count vs the remainder), exactly as the sibling drives.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const cpos: i64 = @as(i64, ceil_pos); // safe: 1 <= ceil_pos <= sample_max, proven above
    const cneg: i64 = @as(i64, ceil_neg); // safe: 1 <= ceil_neg <= sample_max, proven above
    const n: i64 = @as(i64, num);
    const d: i64 = @as(i64, den);
    // invariant: the gain terms are both bounded and positive -- num ≥ den ≥ 1 and num ≤ max_drive_num -- so the pre-gain below is a positive scale
    // that never overflows i64, and both ceilings sit inside the rail.
    assert(n >= d and d >= 1 and n <= @as(i64, drive.max_drive_num) and cpos >= 1 and cpos <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max) and
        cneg >= 1 and cneg <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const dry: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]);
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 -- it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and dry <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // The pre-gain, ALES78's verbatim: dry boosted by num/den in i64, @divTrunc toward zero exactly as ALES6's fader. |dry| ≤ 32768 and
        // num ≤ 2^20, so the product fits i64 with vast headroom and the boosted value carries dry's sign.
        const boosted: i64 = @divTrunc(dry * n, d);
        // ALES78's ceiling law, split by sign: a positive boosted value past +ceil_pos pins there; a negative one past −ceil_neg pins there; one
        // within its half passes at its boosted value. The two ceilings differ, so the clip is ASYMMETRIC -- even harmonics, the tube's voice.
        const pinned: i64 = if (boosted > cpos) cpos else if (boosted < -cneg) -cneg else boosted;
        // invariant: the pinned value lands within [−ceil_neg, ceil_pos] ⊆ [sample_min, sample_max], so the write fits the i16 rail BY
        // CONSTRUCTION -- the clip is the asymmetric pin above, and the saturate that follows is a documented no-op over an already-pinned value.
        assert(pinned >= -cneg and pinned <= cpos);
        // invariant: the sign is held -- a positive boosted sample stays non-negative and a negative one stays non-positive, so the tube clip never
        // FLIPS a sample even as it clips the two halves unevenly. Asymmetry lives in the magnitude, never the sign.
        assert((boosted >= 0 and pinned >= 0) or (boosted <= 0 and pinned <= 0));
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(pinned);
    }
    // invariant: a tube drive changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: EQUAL CEILINGS ARE ALES78's HARD CLIP (the family recognition, proven byte-for-byte against the real hard drive); a HAND-COMPUTED
//     ASYMMETRIC CLIP matches exactly (positive half to ceil_pos, negative to ceil_neg); the map is NOT ODD, the honest even-harmonic signature
//     (tube(1000) = ceil_pos, tube(−1000) = −ceil_neg, ceil_pos ≠ ceil_neg); EACH HALF STAYS UNDER ITS OWN CEILING and the sign is held under a
//     heavy drive; a SYMMETRIC-CEILING drive stays ODD (the degenerate recovers tube(−x) = −tube(x)); the span discipline holds; and each fault --
//     BadGain, BadCeiling (either side zero or over-rail), BadRange -- refuses by name, the clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. EQUAL CEILINGS ARE ALES78's HARD CLIP -- the crux's degenerate. With ceil_pos == ceil_neg the two branches collapse into one ceiling and the
    //    tube drive reproduces the hard drive byte-for-byte, at the same gain, over a signal that both passes and clips. Proven against the REAL
    //    ALES78 (both run).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000, 5000, -6000, 100, -50 };
        var a = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&a, &sig);
        var b = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&b, &sig);
        try tube(&a, 0, a.len, 4, 1, 1500, 1500); // both ceilings equal
        try drive.drive(&b, 0, b.len, 4, 1, 1500); // ALES78's hard clip
        if (!eq(a.samples(), b.samples())) return error.EqualCeilNotHard;
    }

    // 2. A HAND-COMPUTED ASYMMETRIC CLIP -- ceil_pos = 1500, ceil_neg = 800, gain ×2, the two rails read directly. 500->1000 (passes); 1000->1500 (pins
    //    positive, 2000 > 1500); −300->−600 (passes); −600->−800 (pins negative, −1200 < −800); −100->−200 (passes). The two halves clip at DIFFERENT
    //    magnitudes -- the tube's uneven rail.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 500, 1000, -300, -600, -100 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 1500, 800);
        const want = [_]i16{ 1000, 1500, -600, -800, -200 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.AsymmetricWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE MAP IS NOT ODD -- the honest even-harmonic signature. With ceil_pos = 1500 ≠ ceil_neg = 800, a ± symmetric loud input maps to an
    //    ASYMMETRIC output: +1000 boosted ×3 pins to +1500, −1000 boosted ×3 pins to −800, so tube(−x) ≠ −tube(x). The uneven rail no odd clipper
    //    makes -- a DC offset and a full set of even harmonics.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1000, -1000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 3, 1, 1500, 800);
        const out = clip.samples();
        if (out[0] != 1500 or out[1] != -800) return error.EvenSignatureWrong;
        if (out[1] == -out[0]) return error.UnexpectedlyOdd; // −800 ≠ −1500: the map is genuinely not odd
    }

    // 4. EACH HALF STAYS UNDER ITS OWN CEILING, SIGN HELD -- under a heavy ×8 drive every positive output is at most ceil_pos and every negative at
    //    least −ceil_neg, and no sample flips sign. The asymmetry lives in the magnitude, never the sign.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -100, 4000, -4000, 20000, -20000, 5, -5 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const cpos: i16 = 1200;
        const cneg: i16 = 600;
        try tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 8, 1, cpos, cneg);
        const out = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (out[i] > cpos or out[i] < -cneg) return error.CeilingCrossed;
            const s = sig[i];
            if (!((s >= 0 and out[i] >= 0) or (s <= 0 and out[i] <= 0))) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 5. A SYMMETRIC-CEILING DRIVE STAYS ODD -- the degenerate ceil_pos == ceil_neg recovers tube(−x) = −tube(x): a ± symmetric input maps to a ±
    //    symmetric output, so the family's odd members (the hard clip, the overdrive, the wavefolder) are the special case where the two rails meet.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 800, -800, 4000, -4000, 20, -20 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 5, 2, 1200, 1200); // both rails equal — the odd special case
        const out = clip.samples();
        if (out[0] != -out[1] or out[2] != -out[3] or out[4] != -out[5]) return error.EqualNotOdd;
    }

    // 6. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE -- driving a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the whole suite
    //    keeps. Drive the middle two of six with an asymmetric clip; the first and last two are untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 5000, -5000, 300, 400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try tube(&clip, 2, 2, 4, 1, 1000, 700); // indices 2,3 only: 5000→1000, −5000→−700
        const want = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 1000, -700, 300, 400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 7a. A ZERO DENOMINATOR / BELOW-UNITY GAIN / OVERSIZED NUMERATOR each refuse BadGain -- before any write, the clip untouched (ALES78's gain
    //     faults, verbatim).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 0, 500, 500)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroDenTouched;
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1, 2, 500, 500)) |_| return error.BelowUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongBelowUnity;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedBelowUnityTouched;
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, drive.max_drive_num + 1, 1, 500, 500)) |_| return error.WideNumSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongWideNum;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedWideNumTouched;
    }

    // 7b. EITHER CEILING zero or over-rail refuses BadCeiling -- the positive side and the negative side each guarded -- before any write, the clip
    //     untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 0, 500)) |_| return error.ZeroPosSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroPos;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroPosTouched;
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 500, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroNegSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroNeg;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroNegTouched;
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1, 500)) |_| return error.OverPosSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverPos;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverPosTouched;
        if (tube(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 500, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1)) |_| return error.OverNegSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverNeg;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverNegTouched;
    }

    // 7c. A SPAN past the end refuses BadRange -- before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, 10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (tube(&clip, 1, 3, 2, 1, 500, 500)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-tube: the ASYMMETRIC TUBE DRIVE — a different ceiling per sign, the DRIVE family's first CLIP that is not ODD. The hard clip, " ++
        "overdrive, and wavefolder shape the MAGNITUDE and carry the SIGN (odd functions, odd harmonics only); the tube drive breaks that symmetry " ++
        "on purpose — it pins the positive half at ceil_pos and the negative at ceil_neg, and a real tube stage clips harder on one polarity than " ++
        "the other, where its warm EVEN-order harmonics are born. ONE CEILING PER SIGN IS THE PLAINEST ASYMMETRY, and a hard clip is the tube drive " ++
        "whose two ceilings are EQUAL: boosted = divTrunc(x·num, den) (ALES78's pre-gain verbatim), then y = pin(boosted, +ceil_pos, −ceil_neg). " ++
        "Equal ceilings reproduce ALES78's hard drive byte-for-byte (proven against the real hard clip); a hand-computed asymmetric clip matches " ++
        "exactly (ceil_pos 1500, ceil_neg 800, ×2: 500→1000, 1000→1500, −300→−600, −600→−800, −100→−200); the map is NOT odd, tube(1000)=1500 while " ++
        "tube(−1000)=−800, the uneven rail no odd clipper makes (a DC offset and even harmonics); each half stays under its own ceiling and the " ++
        "sign is held under a heavy ×8 drive; a symmetric-ceiling drive stays ODD (the odd members are the special case where the two rails meet); " ++
        "the span discipline holds; a zero den / below-unity gain / oversized numerator refuses BadGain, either ceiling zero or over-rail " ++
        "BadCeiling, a span past the end BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched. The asymmetric pin lands every output in [−ceil_neg, ceil_pos] " ++
        "⊆ the i16 rail BEFORE the write, so the clip is the pin and the saturate a documented no-op; one multiply, one divide, one sign-aware pin " ++
        "per sample; MEMORYLESS; no delay line, no anti-aliasing, no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. " ++
        "The DRIVE family now holds five shapes — the wall, the shoulder, the mirror, the coarse grid, and the uneven rail; a sample-rate " ++
        "decimator is the next\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
